{
  "id": "physics-of-information-systems",
  "type": "research-series",
  "title": "Physics of Information Systems",
  "subtitle": "First-principles essays on the categorical gap between reality and its representations, and what that gap forces every information system to do.",
  "abstract": "A multi-part publication exploring information systems from first principles, organized into three tracks, now complete. Reality traces the categorical gap between a thing and its representation, down through raw signals and observation, to the physics of time itself. Information asks what a system does with what it has observed once the moment of watching has passed — how it retains state, preserves history, works fast without claiming to be true, and how meaning itself only ever emerges from a relationship rather than living inside any single fact. Computation asks what a system is allowed to do with meaning once it has it — the deliberate compressions called models, the real cost of coordinating independent parts, and the decisions all of it was ever built to enable. An epilogue closes the series by tracing the single gap — between what actually is and whatever stands in for it — that every track examined from a different angle. The series draws on measurement theory, information theory, queueing theory, signal processing, distributed-systems theory, statistical learning theory, and game theory — with the formal statements and diagrams shown alongside the essays, not left implicit.",
  "date": "2026-08-01",
  "program": "Data & Information Systems",
  "author": "Shreyas Agarwal",
  "revision": "Revision 07",
